{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2541 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "528cc995888e10ef936f7df33c2ac89b0f95623231c98ca3dbabdbae23fd49c3" :edb/db-before-t 2540 :edb/db-after-t 2541 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535853 50 #inst "2026-10-09T09:02:48.192Z" 13194139535853 true] [17592186124243 1100 "Consider the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/3d11ab5d1681b0aefa6c64c4f6dad9cdf396f61d00ac0b90cb1efa64881c54cc.png) Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186124243 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})$." 13194139535853 false] [17592186124243 1145 "Finding the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSetting $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ we then need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nT\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nFirst, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-\\frac{3}{2})R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y$. That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=-2y$.\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535853 false] [17592186124243 1145 "Finding the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/5fa2e7ff6cfc27fce7698124fb835abf5c6f02221ba7c7ebb0b371fef6d3c15a.png)\n\nSetting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/f9fc80048a348773c656ec98ac9299eb1c611bfb07eee747b883eb7398dc295f.png) we then need to solve the equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/cace77f13c7ef817cfefaab742bdca008b29a177b0783dc761bc24d8a1273af3.png)" 13194139535853 true] [17592186124244 1100 "Given the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/a441d65e864253e32c59c58b6b0684482b6eaee7fd443fdb2f7c026f0fe10d1f.png) find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186124244 1100 "Given the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})$." 13194139535853 false] [17592186124244 1145 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{nullity}(T) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))\n$$\n\nIn order to find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))$, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 3R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot columns. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))=0\n$$" 13194139535853 false] [17592186124244 1145 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/f27d3195556ff8786f9416cddbe4ad52cf1838f2b0ce37b4da11746fee59fb9c.png)\n\nRecall that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{nullity}(T) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot columns. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))=0.\n$$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186124245 1100 "Consider the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/d0a0fabd7275cdceed8c0c62e58bbfc0e5f23322145d551e412877075cbb6e3c.png) and the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/4dd5f4c300c5870e696c5485fffdef1bbd9b7741b23c7d27fda1198e31baa33b.png) Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186124245 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})$." 13194139535853 false] [17592186124245 1145 "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(u) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot k + 12 \\cdot 2 - 3 \\cdot 6 \\\\ -2 \\cdot k + 4 \\cdot 2 - 2 \\cdot 6 \\\\ 6 \\cdot k - 3 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot 6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3k + 6 \\\\ -2k - 4 \\\\ 6k + 12\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{3k + 6 = 0; - 2k - 4 = 0; 6k + 12 = 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2$." 13194139535853 false] [17592186124245 1145 "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot k+12\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 6 \\\\ -2\\cdot k+4\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ 6\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k+6 \\\\ -2k-4 \\\\ 6k+12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3k+6=0 \\\\ -2k-4=0 \\\\ 6k+12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2.$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186124273 1100 "Consider the linear transformation\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the vectors\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$?" 13194139535853 false] [17592186124273 1100 "Consider the linear transformation \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/c3d64ddcfd33db2e96caf6db5d85587857c3715e8d91a7dc8f4801c117fc89b0.png) \nand the vectors \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/4fbbb82838d7fa94fe0aeb47ed48952bfe573e12874d3891f8650a15b46e0ee7.png)\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}?$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186124273 1145 "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(v_{1}) &= T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{2}) &= T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{3}) &= T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1$." 13194139535853 false] [17592186124273 1145 "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0},$ we conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1.$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186124437 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370403 13194139535853 true] [17592186124437 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370417 13194139535853 true] [17592186124437 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370424 13194139535853 true] [17592186124437 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370444 13194139535853 true] [17592186124438 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370451 13194139535853 true] [17592186124438 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370465 13194139535853 true] [17592186124438 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370493 13194139535853 true] [17592186124438 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370500 13194139535853 true] [17592186124439 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370396 13194139535853 true] [17592186124439 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370479 13194139535853 true] [17592186124439 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370486 13194139535853 true] [17592186124467 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370389 13194139535853 true] [17592186124467 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370410 13194139535853 true] [17592186124467 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370431 13194139535853 true] [17592186124467 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370458 13194139535853 true] [17592186124467 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370472 13194139535853 true] [17592186125020 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535853 true] [17592186125020 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4x - 2y \\\\ -10x + 5y\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $u = \\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$. Find the value of $k$ such that $u ∈ Ker(T)$." 13194139535853 false] [17592186125020 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x-2y \\\\ -10x+5y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186125020 1145 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot k-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ -10\\cdot k+5\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4k-12 \\\\ -10k+30\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4k-12=0 \\\\ -10k+30=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=3.$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186125023 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535853 true] [17592186125023 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-8x + 4y \\\\ 6x - 3y \\\\ 2x - y\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $u = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$. Find the value of $k$ such that $u ∈ Ker(T)$." 13194139535853 false] [17592186125023 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-8x+4y \\\\ 6x-3y \\\\ 2x-y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186125023 1145 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-8\\cdot 3+4\\cdot k \\\\ 6\\cdot 3-3\\cdot k \\\\ 2\\cdot 3-k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-24+4k \\\\ 18-3k \\\\ 6-k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-24+4k=0 \\\\ 18-3k=0 \\\\ 6-k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=6.$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186125051 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535853 true] [17592186125051 1100 "Given the linear transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}5x - 7y \\\\ 14y - 10x\\end{bmatrix}$,\n\nfind $3 \\cdot nullity(T) + 1$." 13194139535853 false] [17592186125051 1100 "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-7y \\\\ 14y-10x\\end{aligned}],$\nfind  $3\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+1.$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186125051 1145 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 14\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $2$nd one). \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n3\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+1=3\\cdot 1+1=4.\n$$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186125054 1017 #edb/ref 17592186123346 13194139535853 false] [17592186125054 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370438 13194139535853 true] [17592186125054 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535853 true] [17592186125054 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x - 4y + 2z \\\\ x - 3y + z \\\\ 4x - 4y + z\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $Ker(T)$." 13194139535853 false] [17592186125054 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-4y+2z \\\\ x-3y+z \\\\ 4x-4y+z\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186125054 1145 "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x,$ $y,$ and $z.$ From the third equation, we obtain $z=0$ and from the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we have $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is ${[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370389 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370390 13194139535853 true] [17592186370389 1089 "q-139998" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370389 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535853 true] [17592186370389 1100 "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y \\\\ 6x+3y\\end{aligned}]$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370389 1113 #uuid "4a488962-0aee-5f33-834e-77bf191c7367" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370389 1145 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is ${v}_{2}.$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370390 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370391 13194139535853 true] [17592186370390 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370392 13194139535853 true] [17592186370390 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370393 13194139535853 true] [17592186370390 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370394 13194139535853 true] [17592186370390 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370395 13194139535853 true] [17592186370390 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535853 true] [17592186370390 1041 "selection" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370390 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370395 13194139535853 true] [17592186370390 1110 #uuid "a39085a9-b936-5738-be42-340684e52456" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370391 1034 "${v}_{2}$  and ${v}_{3}$ only" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370391 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535853 true] [17592186370391 1111 #uuid "7fc770bf-0581-5b4b-849f-9ed418837d0b" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370392 1034 "${v}_{3}$ only" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370392 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535853 true] [17592186370392 1111 #uuid "60c85d54-c640-5c24-b63a-5693d5112c16" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370393 1034 "${v}_{1}$  and ${v}_{3}$ only" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370393 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535853 true] [17592186370393 1111 #uuid "f8886185-ef1f-5f17-8bdc-4d2143b80373" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370394 1034 "${v}_{1}$  and ${v}_{2}$ only" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535853 true] [17592186370394 1111 #uuid 