35853 true] [17592186370424 1145 "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{12}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have $x=-z.$ \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]z,\\,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$" 13194139535853 true] [17592186370425 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370426 13194139535853 true] [17592186370425 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370427 13194139535853 true] [17592186370425 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370428 13194139535853 true] [17592186370425 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370429 13194139535853 true] [17592186370425 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370430 13194139535853 true] [17592186370425 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535853 true] [175921863